# Linear Seating Arrangement: Rows, Facing, and Fixed Seats

> Linear seating gives you a row of chairs and a stack of clues. This
> lesson fixes the left-right convention for every facing direction, sorts
> clues into definite, relative, and negative types, and works single-row
> and two-row puzzles end to end.

[important]
Before using any directional clue, find out which way the sitters face.
"Left" and "right" belong to the *person on the page*, not to you. A
north-facing person's left matches your left; a south-facing person's
left is your right — this convention decides half of all marks.
[/important]

## 1. The Left-Right Convention

| Sitter faces | Their LEFT is | Their RIGHT is |
| --- | --- | --- |
| North | your left (west) | your right (east) |
| South | your right (east) | your left (west) |

When a row faces **north**, every "A is to the left of B" clue reads
like a normal page scan; when the row faces **south**, each flips.

[tip]
Write "faces N" or "faces S" in the corner of your diagram the moment
you read it — candidates lose entire puzzles by solving clue 1 in
north-mode and clue 5 in south-mode.
[/tip]

## 2. Three Types of Information

| Clue type | Meaning | Example | Use it |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Definite | fixes a position outright | "G is 3rd from the left" | first |
| Relative | fixes position relative to another person | "A sits immediately right of G" | once an anchor exists |
| Negative | removes possibilities only | "F is not at any end" | last, to break ties |

A well-formed puzzle opens with a definite clue, chains relatives
onto it, and closes with negatives — if your diagram stalls, hunt for
the unused definite clue.

## 3. Setting Up: Count, Blanks, Notation

1. Count the people and draw exactly that many blanks.
2. Number the seats 1 to n from the page's left; mark N or S.
3. Tick off each clue as you place it — a clue used twice creates
false certainty.

[definition]
**Bracket notation**: when a clue links two people, write the pair in a
bracket, such as [A G] for "G immediately right of A" — long chains then
slide along the blanks as one block until locked.
[/definition]

## 4. Worked Example: Seven in a Row Facing North

Seven people A, B, C, D, E, F, G sit in a row facing north.

```text
Clues: G is 3rd from the left end; C is at the extreme right; A is
       immediately right of G; exactly two people sit between A and C;
       E is immediately left of C; B is 2nd from the left; F is not an
       immediate neighbour of B.
Seats:    1   2   3   4   5   6   7
Clue 1-2: -   -   G   -   -   -   C      (definite clues first)
Clue 3:   -   -   G   A   -   -   C      (relative; right = page right)
Clue 4-5: -   -   G   A   -   E   C      (seats 5 and 6 lie between
          A and C; E is immediately left of C = seat 6)
Clue 6-7: D   B   G   A   F   E   C      (F cannot touch B at seat 1,
          so F takes seat 5; D fills the last blank)
```

**Answers:** who sits in the middle? A. F's neighbours? A and E.
Who is second from the right end? E.

## 5. Worked Example: Two Rows Facing Each Other

Ten people sit in two parallel rows of five. Row 1 (top) faces south;
Row 2 (bottom) faces north, and each top person faces a bottom person,
seat for seat.

```text
Clues 1-2: R sits in the middle of Row 1; C faces R -> seats 3 of
   both rows.
Clue 3: S sits immediately LEFT of R — Row 1 faces south, so R's
   left is the page's RIGHT: seat 4.
Clue 4: Q sits immediately RIGHT of R -> the page's LEFT: seat 2.
Clue 5: A sits opposite Q.                    -> Row 2, seat 2.
Clue 6: E sits at the right end of Row 2 — Row 2 faces north, so
   the right end is the page's RIGHT: seat 5.
Clues 7-8: B is at the left end of Row 2 (seat 1; D fills seat 4)
   and T faces E (Row 1, seat 5). P fills Row 1 seat 1.
```

**Result:** Row 1 = P, Q, R, S, T and Row 2 = B, A, C, D, E. Clues 3
and 4 only work through the facing convention: in a south-facing row,
"left of R" means the seat *after* R on the page.

[info title="Why two rows confuse candidates"]
The two rows face each other, so their lefts are mirrored: what is on
the left for Row 1 (facing south) is on the right for Row 2 at the
facing position — translate clues into page positions before combining.
[/info]

## 6. Facing-Variable Questions

Harder puzzles seat some people facing north and others south in the
same row, so each person carries their own left-right frame. If a clue
says "D sits to the left of C" where C faces north, count using C's
frame: C's left is the page's left, so D lies to the page's left of C,
regardless of which way D personally faces.

## 7. Clue Phrasings and Interpretations

| Phrase in the question | Exact interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| "3rd from the left end" | seat 3 counting from the page's left, if facing north |
| "to the immediate right of X" | adjacent seat in X's right direction |
| "second to the left of X" | two seats away in X's left direction |
| "at one of the extreme ends" | seat 1 or seat n — two branches, resolve later |
| "faces X" (two rows) | occupies the seat directly opposite X's seat |
| "as many between A and B as between C and D" | a count equality, not a position |

## 8. Common Traps and Strategy

- **Mixing facing directions:** solving some clues in north-mode,
  others in south-mode, in the same puzzle.
- **Counting yourself, not the sitter:** "left of" in a south-facing
  row is your right.
- **Trusting the first arrangement:** one early branch poisons the
  rest — re-check every clue at the end.
- **Ignoring branches:** "at one of the extreme ends" means two
  branches; carry both until a clue kills one.

| Stage | Move |
| --- | --- |
| Read the stem | note row count, facing direction, total people |
| Phase 1 | draw blanks, number them, place definite clues first |
| Phase 2 | chain relative clues onto placed people |
| Phase 3 | use negative clues to break remaining ties |
| Finish | verify the arrangement against every clue |

[example]
Exam method: **(1)** facing direction first, always, **(2)** blanks and
seat numbers, **(3)** definite clues, **(4)** bracket relative pairs
and slide them as blocks, **(5)** negatives last, **(6)** re-check
every clue. If two arrangements survive, keep both and answer the
questions that agree in both cases first.
[/example]
